use std
into mover.amni

The `mover` library: a capsule character controller on top of `physics` (its Box3D-style mover queries); namespace mover, imports math, vec and physics. Every number is f32 and every point or direction a float3, like physics. Already provided and never defined here: `physics::World`; `physics::Capsule` (fields center1, center2, radius); `physics::Plane` (normal, offset); `physics::CollisionPlane` (plane, push_limit, push, clip_velocity); `physics::PlaneHit` (field plane); `physics::RayHit` (fields point, normal, fraction, hit); `physics::QueryFilter` and `physics::default_query_filter()`; `physics::collide_mover(world, origin, capsule, filter)` giving a growable array of PlaneHit; `physics::cast_mover(world, origin, capsule, translation, filter) f32`, the fraction of the translation that is free (1 when nothing is hit); `physics::solve_planes(delta, planes)` taking a mutable slice of CollisionPlane and returning the solved delta and an iteration count; `physics::clip_vector(v, planes)` taking a read-only slice; and `physics::cast_shape(world, origin, points, radius, translation, filter)` giving a growable array of RayHit, nearest first, with `point` in world space. In every query the capsule, the points and the planes are relative to the origin, and the mover always passes its own feet position `pos` as the origin.

`Settings` holds radius 0.3, height 1.8, step_height 0.35, max_slope 0.87 (radians, about 50 degrees), max_speed 4.7, accelerate 10, air_steer 0.2, friction 7, stop_speed 1.7, jump_speed 5.2, gravity 9.8, rise_speed 4.7, skin 0.01 and `filter`, a `physics::QueryFilter` (the default one). `settings() Settings` returns those defaults.

`Mover` holds `pos` (the feet: the ground point under the character when standing), `vel`, `grounded` (bool), `ground_normal`, `s` (a Settings), `planes` (a growable array of `physics::CollisionPlane` from the last move) and `hits` (int, how many planes the last step touched). `new(pos, s) Mover` starts at pos at rest, not grounded, with ground normal straight up, no planes and 0 hits.

`capsule(m) physics::Capsule`, relative to the feet: center1 (0, step_height + radius, 0), center2 (0, height - radius, 0), radius plus skin (the skin keeps the solver's contact slop outside the real radius). `walkable(n, s) bool` is true when n.y is at least cos(max_slope).

`gather(w, m, standing)` with m by reference: clears `m.planes` and, for every hit of `collide_mover` at `m.pos` with the capsule and the settings' filter, pushes a CollisionPlane with that plane, push_limit 3.4e38, push 0 and clip_velocity true. When standing is true and the plane is not walkable but its normal points up (y above 0) and the horizontal part (x, 0, z) is longer than 1e-4, the plane is first flattened into a wall: its normal becomes that horizontal part normalized and its offset is divided by the horizontal part's length, so a steep slope blocks like a wall instead of being climbed.

`slide(w, m, delta, standing)` with m by reference moves the feet toward `m.pos + delta`: up to 5 rounds of `gather`, then `solve_planes` of (target minus `m.pos`) over all of `m.planes`, then, when standing is false, the solved delta's y is cut to at most the larger of 0 and the y of what was asked (so pushing into a steep slope in the air never lifts the mover), then `cast_mover` of the solved delta from `m.pos`, and `m.pos` moves by the solved delta times that fraction; the rounds stop early when that move is shorter than 0.001. Afterwards `m.vel` becomes `clip_vector` of itself over `m.planes` and `m.hits` is the number of planes.

`probe(w, m) bool, f32, float3` looks for ground under the feet: a sphere of radius 0.9 × radius centered at (0, step_height + radius, 0) relative to the feet is cast straight down by step_height × 3 + 0.1 × radius with `cast_shape`. With no hit it returns false, 0 and straight up. Otherwise, taking the nearest hit with normal n and point p: when n.y is above 0.1 the ground height under the feet is p.y minus (n.x × (pos.x - p.x) + n.z × (pos.z - p.z)) / n.y, else p.y; it returns true, that height minus `m.pos.y` and n.

`step(w, m, wish, jump, dt)` with m by reference: remember `was` = `m.grounded`. Take the horizontal velocity h = (vel.x, 0, vel.z). When was: friction, where the speed is the length of h; below 0.01 h becomes zero, else h is scaled by max(speed - max(speed, stop_speed) × friction × dt, 0) / speed. The wish uses only its x and z, with length clamped to 1; when that length is above 1e-4 accelerate Quake-style: the desired speed is max_speed × length along the wish direction; add = desired speed - dot(h, direction); when add is above 0, h grows along the direction by min(add, accelerate × max_speed × dt × (1 when was, else air_steer)). Then vel gets h's x and z. Vertical: when was and jump, vel.y = jump_speed and `m.grounded` becomes false; else when was, vel.y = 0; else vel.y drops by gravity × dt. Then `slide` by vel × dt, standing when was and not jumping. Then `probe`: when it hits, the normal is walkable, vel.y is at most 0, and the height dy is between minus (step_height × 2 when was and not jumping, else 0.02) and step_height + 0.1 × radius, the mover is grounded: `ground_normal` is that normal, vel.y = 0, and the feet move vertically by dy (when dy is positive and was, by at most rise_speed × dt; landing from the air takes all of dy at once) through `cast_mover` from `m.pos` with the capsule, moving by the free fraction of it. Otherwise grounded is false and the ground normal straight up.
